{"text": "Mark A. Davalos\n\nObjective\n\nEducation\n\nProfessional\nExperience\n\nMy career goal as an educator isto assist all youth in reaching their academic potential while\nbecoming lifelong learners directed at improving al quality of life. I truly believe that our\nschools can be the best part of our communities. Every parent and every student wants success\nand we must want the same. The school district is a huge part of how every child grows up and\n\\ve are given that honor each and every day to help build dreams into reality. I believe that all\nstudents can achieve and we must reduce learning gaps for those who struggle. l am a dedicated\nand lifelong educator who believes in public education and would offer knowledge, mentoring,\ndetermination, and vision towards helping others realize our educational vision,\n\nContinuing Administrator? Initial Superintendent License; 2000-2005\nPortland State University, Portland, OR; Valid through 2015\n\nOregon Basic Administrative License; 1998-2000\nPortland State University, Portland, OR\n\nM Ed; Educational Management; 1993-1996\nUniversity of La Verne, La Verne, CA\n\nCA Preliminary Administrative Credential; 1996\nUniversity of La Verne, La Verne, CA\n\nB.A., Social Science; 1971-1975\nUniversity of La Verne, La Verne, CA\n\nSuperintendent; Saint Helens School District; Saint Helens, OR July 2011-Present\nSupervision of 6 Schools (All Grades K-12)\nHiring excellent teachers and leaders (Human Resource Management)\nWorking collaboratively with teachers unions, communities and administrators (HRM)\nProblem-solving high stress community situations (Communication Leader)\nEquity and fairness in distribution of resources (Budget Management)\nProviding support to developing leaders (Leadership)\nSystemic school improvement(Teaching & Learning Management)\nAssisting low performing schools to increase student achievement\nRemoving barriers for students moving through the K-12 system to increase graduation rates,\nDesigning and implementing innovative programs based on best practices\n\nDeputy Superintendent; July 2009-July 2011; Portland Public Sehoo! District, Portland, OR\nSupervision of 28 K-12 Schoolsin Region 2\nProblem-solving high stress community situations\nEquity and faimess in distribution of resources\nProviding support to developing leaders,\n\u2018Successful systemic school improvement, (AYP, Data Teams, PLC, RTI and Data Wise)\nAssisting low performing schools to increase student achievement\nRemoving barriers for students moving through the K-12 system\nDesigning and implementing innovative programs\nHiring excellent leaders,\n\u2018Working collaboratively with teachers unions, communities and administrators\nEvaluate, Assign and Hire Administrators",
